5. Simulation Protocol and Analysis

100 independent runs per city, 10 simulated years (3 650 ticks per run). For each hypothesis, the primary statistical tests are:

· H1 & H3: Mixed‑effects models with agent‑level random intercepts, fixed effects of Security (continuous), city (categorical), time (continuous), and their interactions. Trust stability is operationalised as the standard deviation of trust over the final 12 months.
· H2: Within‑Paris and within‑Rome comparisons of mean trust 12 months before versus 12 months after the simulated certification date, using paired t‑tests aggregated across runs. The trust‑fragility window is the difference in mean trust between the August 2026 and December 2027 scenarios (see Section 7.4).
· H4: Independent‑samples t‑test comparing dependency ratio at year 10 between Socratic‑intervention and control agents aged 65+ in Rome and Paris. Secondary analysis: Cox proportional hazards model on time‑to‑dependency‑threshold (dependency ratio > 0.9).
· H5: Cox proportional hazards model with robot exposure as a time‑varying covariate, stratified by city and sector, with censoring at simulated death or emigration.

Sensitivity analyses perturb all free parameters by ±15 % and repeat the core runs. The Kalman‑filter trust alternative (Section 8.4) will be run in parallel; if the hypotheses hold under both distributional assumptions, the cultural effects are considered robust.

