9. Model Formalisation and Implementation Details

This section provides the complete mathematical specification of all agent‑level mechanisms, ensuring full transparency and reproducibility. The model is a discrete‑time stochastic ABM with tick granularity of one day. The full source code, parameter files, and experiment scripts will be released under an MIT licence.

9.1 Agent State Vector

Each human agent i at time t is defined by the tuple:

S_{i,t} = \langle \text{age}_i, \text{edu}_i, \text{sector}_i, \mathbf{v}_i, \text{anx}_{i,t}, \{\alpha_{i,r,t}, \beta_{i,r,t}\}_{r \in R}, \text{aut}_{i,t}, \text{dep}_{i,t}, \text{msens}_i, \text{inoc}_{i,t}, \text{emp}_{i,t} \rangle

where:

· v_i is a 10‑dimensional Schwartz value vector, normalised to [0,1] per dimension, centred on the individual’s ipsatised mean.
· anx_i,t ∈ [0,1] is the current anxiety level, initialised from the RAS distribution and modified by interactions.
· α_i,r,t, β_i,r,t are the Beta trust parameters for robot type r (logistics, social, domestic, healthcare).
· aut_i,t ∈ [0,100] is perceived autonomy (percent).
· dep_i,t ∈ [0,1] is the dependency ratio, defined as the proportion of daily decisions delegated to robots over the preceding 30 days.
· msens_i ∼ N(μ_ms, σ²_ms) is the individual media‑sensitivity coefficient, drawn from a distribution calibrated to match the effect‑size heterogeneity in Brauner et al. (2026).
· inoc_i,t ∈ {0,1} is the Socratic inoculation flag.
· emp_i,t ∈ {employed, unemployed, separated} is employment status.

Each robot agent j is defined by its type r_j, autonomy level a_j ∈ [0,1], error‑acknowledgment probability e_j ∼ Beta(a_e, b_e), true reliability ρ_j ∈ [0.7, 0.99], and EU AI Act certification status c_j,t ∈ {0,1} (time‑varying).

9.2 Interaction Protocol and Trust Update

On each day, each employed agent interacts with n_int robots drawn from their sector. Each interaction consists of a binary task (success/failure) drawn from Bernoulli(ρ_j). The trust weight w is computed as:

w_{i,r,t} = w_0 \cdot (1 + \lambda_{\text{err}} \cdot e_{j}) \cdot (1 + \lambda_{\text{reg}} \cdot c_{j,t}) \cdot \exp(-\gamma \cdot \text{anx}_{i,t} \cdot (1 + \text{Security}_i)) \cdot (1 + \delta_{i,r} \cdot \mathbb{I}_{\text{bias}})

where:

· Security_i is the agent’s Schwartz Security value (centred).
· γ = 0.8 (anxiety discount steepness).
· δ_i,r = 0.2 if age_i ≥ 65 and robot type is social or healthcare, else 0.05.
· The automation‑bias indicator 𝕀_bias is drawn from Bernoulli(p_bias) with p_bias = 0.15 for elderly agents interacting with social/healthcare robots, and 0.02 otherwise. If triggered and the outcome is a failure, α is incremented by w instead of β (self‑blame misattribution).

Trust parameters update:

\alpha_{i,r,t+1} = \alpha_{i,r,t} + w_{i,r,t} \cdot O_t\\
\beta_{i,r,t+1} = \beta_{i,r,t} + w_{i,r,t} \cdot (1 - O_t)

with O_t = 1 for success, 0 for failure (except under automation bias where a failure may be coded as O_t = 1). Mean trust for robot type r is T̄_i,r,t = α/(α+β).

9.3 Anxiety Dynamics

Anxiety is updated daily:

\text{anx}_{i,t+1} = \text{clamp}\Big( \text{anx}_{i,t} + \eta \cdot f_{\text{exp},t} \cdot (1 + 0.5 \cdot \text{Security}_i) - \zeta \cdot F_{i,t} - \mu \cdot \bar{c}_{i,t}, \ 0, \ 1 \Big)

where:

· f_exp,t ∈ {0,1,2} is the number of robot interactions that day.
· F_i,t = 1 - exp(-κ · N_success,i,t) is a familiarity logistic with κ = 0.05, and N_success the cumulative successful interactions.
· c̄_i,t ∈ {0,1} is the proportion of interacting robots that are EU‑certified that day.
· η = 0.02, ζ = 0.03, μ = 0.04.

9.4 Autonomy Degradation

Perceived autonomy declines as robot density in the agent’s sector d_sector,t (robots per 1 000 workers) increases:

\text{aut}_{i,t} = 100 \cdot \exp\left( - \phi \cdot \frac{d_{\text{sector},t}}{d_{\text{baseline}}} \right) \cdot (1 + 0.5 \cdot \bar{c}_{i,t})

where φ = 0.01, d_baseline is the initial density, and the certification buffer halfs the degradation rate.

9.5 Socratic Intervention Mechanism

The trigger condition is evaluated each tick for agents aged ≥ 60:

\text{trigger}_{i,t} = \mathbb{I}\left( \max_{r} \bar{T}_{i,r,t} > \theta_{\text{over}} \ \land \ \text{dep}_{i,t} > 0.7 \ \text{for} \ k \ \text{consecutive days} \ \land \ \text{inoc}_{i,t}=0 \right)

When triggered:

· α_i,r,t reset to 1, β_i,r,t reset to 5 for all robot types (skeptical prior, mean trust ≈0.167).
· inoc_i,t set to 1 for 365 days.
· δ_i,r halved (δ ← δ/2) permanently.
· dep_i,t is reduced by 0.3 (immediate behavioural effect) and then follows the standard update thereafter.

9.6 Media Shock Implementation

At the start of each month, a city‑level media event is drawn: positive (prob = 0.4), negative (0.3), or none (0.3). The shock magnitude for each agent is:

\Delta \text{benefit}_i = \text{msens}_i \cdot \beta_{\text{pos,benefit}} \quad \text{or} \quad \Delta \text{risk}_i = \text{msens}_i \cdot \beta_{\text{neg,risk}}

with β values taken from Brauner et al. (2026): β_pos,benefit = 0.52; β_neg,risk = -0.69. The shock decays exponentially with half‑life 15 days, modulating the weight w via:

w_{i,r,t} \leftarrow w_{i,r,t} \cdot \exp( \Delta_{i,\text{current}} )

where Δ_current is the residual shock on that day.

9.7 Labour‑Market Transitions

Each day, employed agents face a separation probability:

p_{\text{sep},i,t} = p_{\text{base, sector}} + \beta_{\text{robot}} \cdot \Delta d_{\text{sector},t} + \beta_{\text{EPL}} \cdot \text{EPL\_index}_{\text{city}} + \beta_{\text{age}} \cdot \text{age}_i

· β_robot = +0.02 for New York service sectors, −0.01 for Paris/Rome moderate‑labour‑cost sectors (complementarity).
· EPL_index_city: Rome 2.76 (OECD), Paris 2.82, New York 1.17.
· β_EPL = −0.005 (higher EPL reduces separations).
· p_base,sector calibrated from Eurostat / BLS separation rates.

Separated agents may become re‑employed after d days with probability proportional to a re‑employment rate that decreases with the duration of unemployment, creating hysteresis.

